in @prism

in @mirror/shard
in @epistemologic/reality/silicon/compute_bound

# @scheduler: the Scheduler Tower's substrate primitives.
#
# Where @mirror/runtime/gen_prism is the actor, @mirror/shard is the
# observer-relative deployment manifest, and @epistemologic/silicon/
# compute_bound carries the budget envelope, @scheduler exposes the
# load-topology-derived primitives the Tower reads at runtime.
#
# v1.5 of the Scheduler Tower (docs/specs/scheduler-tower.md) covers
# the demand contract, dispatcher strategies, and the KMS
# temperature β. This file lands one primitive: the reduction
# budget. The budget IS the load-topology snapshot @epistemologic/
# property/halts clause (b) reads when verifying termination. The
# rest of the Tower's demand-contract surface lives on gen_prism
# itself (the extension landed in tick S-1; see the scheduler-tower
# spec §2).
#
# Per the 2026-05-25 substrate decision (Alex): halting has two
# conditions joined by disjunction:
#
#   (a) autopoietic settlement (§clause-a in halts.mirror), and
#   (b) reduction exhaustion (§clause-b in halts.mirror).
#
# Clause (b) is structurally bounded by `reduction_budget(shard)`.
# This grammar declares that primitive. Bodies are Fate-resolved
# holes; the tournament reads the shard's compute_bound at runtime,
# weights by current load (the Scheduler's queue depths per the
# Tower spec §3), and returns the per-tick reductions ceiling. Same
# shard + same load fingerprint = same budget; the budget is
# deterministic against its inputs and content-addressable through
# the fragmentation cache (per shard-design.md Q3).
#
# Spec: docs/specs/scheduler-tower.md.
# Insight: docs/insights/2026-05-25-gram-and-mirror-same-architecture-
# two-altitudes.md (GRAM's ACT ↔ reduction budget at sub-Turing
# altitude).

grammar @scheduler {

  # ── reduction_budget: load-topology-derived ceiling ───────────────
  #
  # The per-tick reduction ceiling for trajectories that run inside
  # this shard. Reads from `s.compute.max_reductions` (the hardware
  # carrier; per @epistemologic/silicon/compute_bound) and weights
  # by current load (queue depths, in-flight demand windows, β).
  #
  # Return is u64 (raw reductions, not @time.monotonic; reductions
  # are countable events, not durations). The unit lines up with
  # the Scheduler's demand_window.capacity convention (§10.2 of the
  # scheduler-tower spec resolves "units are messages"; reductions
  # are the per-tick analog inside one gen_prism).
  #
  # Body is a Fate-resolved hole. The tournament picks the
  # weighting strategy that maximises throughput against the
  # shard's load topology; same shard + same load fingerprint =
  # same budget. None of the weighting logic lives in Rust; the
  # substrate pulls it into grammar.
  #
  # @epistemologic/property/halts clause (b) reads this primitive
  # to verify termination. The property's `reductions_bounded`
  # action walks the type's tick map, counts reductions per tick,
  # and confirms the per-trajectory total stays below the budget
  # this primitive returns.
  reduction_budget(s: shard) -> u64 { \ }
}

out reduction_budget
out @scheduler
